The city manager shall appoint and discharge all employees subject to the conditions of these policies and shall be responsible for the administration of the personnel policies and rules contained within which shall apply to all appointed employees except those specifically exempted. The city manager may assign a designated representative to assist with the administration of this policy. He shall continually monitor and evaluate the effectiveness of the compensation plan and shall recommend appropriate changes and revisions at any time necessary. At the time of budget preparation each year, he shall evaluate the entire pay structure, personnel salaries, pay scale and pay policies, and shall recommend appropriate changes and revisions to the mayor and city council as necessary.
The city manager or his or her designated representative shall:
(1) Maintain a position classification plan;
(2) Develop and submit a pay plan for annual budget consideration;
(3) Publicize job vacancies in an effort to obtain the most qualified applicants;
(4) Take job applications and make information available to the proper supervisor;
(5) Be notified of all selections for hiring;
(6) Maintain appropriate central personnel records; and
(7) Recommend amendments and changes to the personnel policies.
(Ord. No. 2020-023, § 1, 12-14-2020)

492:
The city manager shall be responsible for administering the compensation plan and setting personnel salaries in accordance with guidelines and policies established by the city council. The following factors shall be considered in determining salaries:
(1) Competency of the individual;
(2) Training and experience of the individual;
(3) Training and expertise required for the job;
(4) Salaries paid and fringe benefits provided to comparable municipal, county, state and private sector employees;
(5) Change in the cost of living in the area during the fiscal year;
(6) Any applicable state and federal laws and/or regulations; and
(7) The financial condition of the city.
(Ord. No. 2020-023, § 1, 12-14-2020)

525:
The City of Emerson believes that employees should have the opportunity to enjoy time away from work to help balance their work and personal lives. The city recognizes that employees have diverse needs for time off from work. Because of this, the city has established leave practices to address the balance, as well as to protect the financial well-being of employees during certain absences from work. Employees are responsible for managing their leave balance to allow for adequate reserves to cover unanticipated events requiring time away from work.
Except as provided otherwise in this article, the following apply to all paid leave benefits:
(1) Approval of leave is subject to operational requirements of the department and any request for paid leave may be denied or rescheduled due to staffing needs;
(2) Paid leave shall not be advanced;
(3) All requests for leave must be submitted in writing with as much notice as possible;
(4) Except for military leave, any employee in an unpaid leave status will not accrue paid time off. This will not, however, constitute a break in service for accrual rate purposes;
(5) Employees shall record paid leave in quarter hour increments.
(Ord. No. 2020-023, § 1, 12-14-2020)

526:
Employees will receive the following paid holidays:
(1) New Year's Day;
(2) Martin Luther King's birthday;
(3) President's Day;
(4) Good Friday;
(5) Memorial Day;
(6) Fourth of July;
(7) Labor Day;
(8) Veterans Day;
(9) Thanksgiving Day;
(10) Day after Thanksgiving;
(11) Christmas Eve;
(12) Christmas Day;
(13) Floating holiday—employee's birthday.
Other days may also be designated as paid holidays at the discretion of the mayor and city council. Whenever a holiday falls on Saturday, the preceding Friday shall be designated as substitute holiday and observed as the official holiday for that year. When the holiday falls on Sunday, the following Monday shall be designated as the official holiday for that year. The employee's floating holiday for their birthday must be taken within 30 days of the employee's birthday. The employee is required to obtain approval with their respective department head for the date which they desire to observe their floating holiday.
An employee who is not on approved leave and fails to report on a scheduled workday before or after a holiday shall not be paid for the holiday. Holidays which occur during approved annual or sick leave shall not be charged to annual or sick leave.
All regular full-time employees, including probationary employees hired as full time, that are not required or authorized to work on a council-approved holiday shall receive eight hours pay.
Any regular, full-time employee, including probationary employees hired as full time, that are required to work on a council-approved holiday shall receive pay for each hour worked plus eight hours of holiday pay.
(Ord. No. 2020-023, § 1, 12-14-2020)
